00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:23.659
David: Pester brides?
Narrator: No, pesticides. They're any chemical

00:01:23.659 --> 00:01:27.079
intended to prevent, repel, lessen, or destroy
pests.

00:01:27.079 --> 00:01:33.049
David: Right, pests. Meaning tiny penguins.
Narrator: Well, in this case, sure, but pests

00:01:33.049 --> 00:01:38.540
aren't just penguins. Pests are any unwanted
organism. Pests can spread disease, compete

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:42.979
for resources with other organisms, cause
property and crop damage, or just gross us

00:01:42.979 --> 00:01:43.649
out.
[David moves very close to the screen]

00:01:43.649 --> 00:01:47.030
David: LIKE MICRO-PENGUINS!
Narrator: You know it, tiger.

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:51.799
Narrator: Basically, a pest is an organism
we don't want around for one reason or another.

00:01:51.799 --> 00:01:56.869
These can be insects, rodents, weeds, mildew,
or even bacteria. It's not just good to

00:01:56.869 --> 00:02:01.020
get rid of pests that bother us in the house;
sometimes pests can pose a major health risk

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.259
to us. Hey, David?
David: Oh? We back to me now?

00:02:05.259 --> 00:02:09.560
Narrator: What animal would you guess is responsible
for the most human deaths every year?

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:12.530
David: Penguins!
Narrator: Oh, be serious.

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:16.959
David: I don't know, sharks?
Narrator: Sure, some people are afraid of

00:02:16.959 --> 00:02:19.290
them, but sharks only kill a few people a
year.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:23.459
David: [sarcastic tone] Gee, how about a cow?
Narrator: You're getting closer, cows kill

00:02:23.459 --> 00:02:28.670
a couple dozen people a year.
David: Woah, really? Uh, panda?  

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:32.930
Narrator: this isn' fun anymore, it's
the mosquito. Mosquitos can kill and make

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:37.930
millions of people very ill every year.
David: What?! How can a tiny thing like that

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:41.030
off so many people?
Narrator: Well mosquitos can carry diseases

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:46.440
like malaria, dengue fever, encephalitis,
West Nile virus, and yellow fever to name

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:50.190
a few. Many of these diseases can lead to
death, if untreated.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:54.090
David: Oh my gosh! Well now's the time for
pesticides!

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:58.030
Narrator: In this case, sure they could be
helpful. In fact, the use of pesticides against

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:02.400
health threats like mosquitos has likely saved
millions more lives than mosquitos take every

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.140
year.
David: Take that you abominable arthropod!

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:10.920
Narrator: But it's not just sprays. Pesticides
can be found as baits, powders, collars, disinfectants

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:15.799
like bleach, mold and mildew sprays, weed
killers, swimming pool chemicals, larger animal

00:03:15.799 --> 00:03:19.750
repellents. All of these pesticides you could
probably find on a normal day of shopping!

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.260
Clerk: Sir, do you have a bonus card?
David: No, thank you.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:26.900
Clerk: Would you like to apply for one? I
just need your e-mail, social security code,

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.290
and genetic fingerprint.
David: What's wrong with you people?

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:32.730
Narrator: So David, how do you think that
spray you're planning on using works?

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:37.049
David: I don't know. It just like goes like *pfffffffffff*
then the penguins are all like *gaaaahhhhh

00:03:37.049 --> 00:03:43.569
wahhhhhhh* and then I can watch TV without
the living room smelling like fish.

00:03:43.569 --> 00:03:46.900
Narrator: Not quite, sorry friend.
David: Well, why should I care? This stuff

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:50.030
only works on penguins anyway.
Narrator: That's where a lot of people would

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:54.269
be surprised. Some pesticides only affect
their target species, but many more cause

00:03:54.269 --> 00:03:58.170
similar reactions in all organisms. This means
it's critical to understand what's going

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:02.739
on inside that bottle. Here, look at this.
David: Oh,cool it's a science thing 

00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:07.829
Narrator: That's a chemical called �Chlorpyrifos�.
It's a common ingredient in many pesticides.

00:04:07.829 --> 00:04:11.650
David: So what does it do besides scare kids
in chemistry class?

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:16.140
Narrator: Well, let's look at this chemical
at work. These are hornworm caterpillars.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:20.130
They cause major crop damage to tomato and
tobacco plants across the United States every

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:22.660
year.
David: That's ok, tomatoes are for hippies.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:26.930
Narrator: That statement explains so much
about you. Anyway, if we spray this field

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:30.170
with chlorpyrifos, the chemical will coat
the leaves these caterpillars are eating.

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:34.600
David: Ah, a nice, light vinaigrette!
Narrator: Sure, except this salad dressing

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:39.540
packs a bit more of a kick. When the caterpillars
eat chlorpyrifos, the chemical blocks the

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:43.080
ability for their nerves to switch off. A
simple signal from the brain to make a muscle

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:47.730
flex turns into a signal that can't be shut
off. Essentially, these animals can't turn

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:51.970
their nerves off and the signals become nonsense.
This will cause their nervous systems to eventually

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:54.730
fail, and they die.
David: Well that's awesome! Now all the

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:56.930
caterpillars are gone, so we can get more
food!

00:04:56.930 --> 00:05:01.350
Narrator: Sure, but there is a problem. Many
pesticides, like chlorpyrifos do their jobs

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:05.440
so well, that they can have the same effects
on other organisms, even humans.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:10.510
David: Ok, so I won't lick it, no big deal.
Narrator: You don't always have to lick

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:14.970
it. Some pesticides can get into our body
from being ingested or eaten, but others can

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:20.290
be inhaled or even touched. In fact, chlorpyrifos
is moderately toxic to humans, and can be

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:25.710
absorbed through our skin.
David: [concerned tone] Skin you say, what,

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:32.270
theoretically, could happen if a human maybe
kinda-sorta touches chlorpyrifos?

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:38.520
Narrator: Well, the effect of chemical exposure
depends on 1) How toxic the chemical is 2)

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:42.440
how much exposure you've had and 3) how
you were exposed to the chemical.

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:46.510
Narrator: In the case of chlorpyrifos, it
has the same effect in the human body as it

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:50.460
does in an insect's body. Depending on how
severe the exposure, you could exhibit watery

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:55.870
eyes, drooling [groaning sounds], coughing
[coughing sounds], stomach pain [groaning

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:59.230
sounds], vomiting [vomiting sounds], or diarrhea
[David runs off screen shouting �uh-oh�uh-oh�uh-oh�uh-oh!!!�oh

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:02.570
no! Oh no�no� no�no!�]. In severe
cases, you could experience tremors and muscles

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:07.570
spasms, paralysis, seizures, or even comas.
Narrator: All of this depends on the kind

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:11.670
of pesticide you've been exposed to, and
your level of exposure. Each kind of pesticide

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:15.720
comes with its own properties and potential
health risks. Remember, constant exposure

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:19.930
to a mild pesticide could be a greater risk
to you than a small dose of a highly toxic

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:22.120
pesticide.
Narrator: Meaning, you could touch a very

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:25.650
toxic pesticide accidentally one time, and
your health risk may not be as great as someone

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:29.820
who is in a field spraying a more mild pesticide
every day.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:36.740
David: [Groan] Ok, don't lick, touch, or
breathe too close to the leaves. No problem.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:39.520
Narrator: But you were going to eat the tomato,
right?

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:42.180
David: Well, *I'M* not going to eat the
tomato.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:45.710
Narrator: Ok, SOMEONE will potentially eat
that tomato, and it's been covered with

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:52.060
pesticide. Apples, pears, grapes, strawberries,
lettuce, and many other foods could still

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:55.050
be covered with pesticides when you pick them
up in the grocery store.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:58.280
David: All the more reason to stick to potato
chips and bacon.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:02.160
Narrator: Yeah, because that doesn't come
with any health risks. The easiest way to

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.020
avoid pesticides on your food is to make sure
you thoroughly rinse your produce before eating

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:08.600
anything.
Narrator: good to see you taking a moderate

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:11.600
approach to this, David.
David: Hey, did you see what happened to me

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:16.930
in the last scene? No chances. I was so sick,
I turned blue!

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:21.480
Narrator: Uh,well, you're always,yeah,
I guess I won't get into that now.  

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:25.400
Narrator: Let's not forget, though, pesticides
aren't just sprayed on fields. There are

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:28.370
plenty in your own home. We need to practice
safety here too.

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:34.010
David: Hey, I do all right. Look! All my pesticides
are on the same shelf! And they're out of

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:37.140
reach of the children  that I don't have[looks
sad, then snaps back to normal]

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:41.130
Narrator: That's a start, but it is critical
to ALWAYS follow the exact instructions on

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:43.530
the bottle.
David: Sure, everything's on this label

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:46.690
up until it says Caution who's ever
needed THAT part

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:50.390
Narrator: If you come in contact with a pesticide,
follow the label's instructions. Different

00:07:50.390 --> 00:07:54.450
pesticides will require different treatments!
If you think someone has been poisoned, contact

00:07:54.450 --> 00:08:01.190
the poison control center at (800)222-1222.
Narrator: and lastly, ALWAYS make sure pesticides

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:04.730
are in their ORIGINAL containers. Never transfer
them to some other container.

00:08:04.730 --> 00:08:08.030
David: [David looks at milk bottle with green
fumes coming out of it] Oh We probably

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:11.270
don't need to talk about this one then
[nervous laugh]

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:16.710
David: So, with responsible use, pesticides
can help us control the spread of disease,

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:21.690
protect our native environment and property,
and get rid of nasty things in our house.

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:25.090
Pesticides are pretty great, huh?
Narrator: Well, yes and no.

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:27.860
Narrator: [annoyed tone] *sigh* Is anything
simple with you?

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:32.310
Narrator: Hear me out. So, pesticides can
be very effective against certain pests, but

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:36.409
you will never kill ALL of them.
David: Well, sure, but that's just two of

00:08:36.409 --> 00:08:38.810
them.
Narrator: Ok, but why do you think they survived?

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:41.180
David: I don't know, they go to yoga every
night?

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:46.210
Narrator: They survived because there was
a variation in this population of caterpillars.

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:50.110
These caterpillars possess some trait that
allows them to survive better than others.

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:54.240
This trait that makes them immune to the pesticide
can be likely passed on to their offspring,

00:08:54.240 --> 00:09:00.280
so that means their babies will be
David: [Gasps] Super ultra-mega caterpillars!

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:02.360
[looks terrified] [giant caterpillar roars
loudly in the background]

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:04.640
Narrator: Well, more likely to survive the
spray, yes. So that means

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:09.490
David: WE NEED MORE SPRAY! MORE TOXIC SPRAY!
GET 'IM! [giant caterpillar roars in the

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:10.960
background]
Narrator: Unfortunately, yes. This is called

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:16.830
the "pesticide treadmill". As pests become
immune, we have to use more and deadlier pesticide.

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:21.020
In the 1950s, pests destroyed about 7 percent
of crops grown in the United States. Today,

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:25.280
pests devour closer to 13 percent of US crops.
[giant caterpillar roars in the background]

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:27.360
David: Wait, that's not very convincing
math. [giant caterpillar roars in the background] 

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:31.050
Narrator: Not really, no. Let's also not
forget that pesticides don't just impact

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:33.700
the target species, but anything they come
in contact with.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:35.590
Narrator: What's below you in the ground
right now?

00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:40.730
David: uh, Dinosaurs.
Narrator: I mean, maybe, but how about earthworms? 

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:44.210
Earthworms create tunnels through the soil
which introduce water and air to the soil.

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:48.300
This is critical to the health of a field.
However, the pesticide will kill the earthworms

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:50.760
too.
David: Well, that doesn't make me very sad.

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:54.050
They don't even have faces.
Narrator: Well it should make you sad, you

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:59.270
species-ist. I basically just told you the
worms help your food grow. It's not just

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:03.370
worms though. Birds and other natural predators
can be killed by the pesticides.

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:07.120
David: Well, that just sounds like we're
going to need even MORE pesticides.

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:11.240
Narrator: Yep, without the predators to help,
there will be even more pests that need to

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:13.760
be taken care of.
Narrator: Even with all this pesticide, only

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:18.260
a small amount of the pesticide actually reaches
the pests. Meaning most of it covers the plants

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:22.110
and the ground. When the rains come, it will
wash the majority of the pesticides into the

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:26.430
rivers and streams, where it could eventually
get in contact with us. This stuff can get

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:29.230
EVERYWHERE!
David: Much as I hate to admit it, I'm not

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:33.880
sure I want to go around my house spraying
pesticides anymore. What else could I do?

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:37.400
Narrator: Well, in your house, pests are like
us, they need food and water to survive. [Sound

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:40.190
of David fixing his leaky faucet] So if you
make sure that all of your food is secured

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:43.910
and put away, there's no reason for them
to show up. You also want to eliminate any

00:10:43.910 --> 00:10:48.110
puddles or leaky faucets so pests can't
get a drink or lay their eggs in the water.

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:52.250
Narrator: Biological controls are also becoming
very popular ways to control pests that typically

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:58.520
target only the pest itself.
David: Yeah biological controls, yep,what,   

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:03.480
Narrator: Biological controls use naturally
occurring chemicals. These often target specific

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:07.330
organisms and occur naturally in the environment.
Narrator: Many insects communicate and attract

00:11:07.330 --> 00:11:11.340
each other using chemicals called "pheromones".
Some traps use these pheromones to attract:

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:16.050
only one kind of insect. You can also plant
cedar trees or use cedar chips in your garden.

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:19.350
The chemicals produced by these trees naturally
repel many pests.

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:23.170
Narrator: You can also go the non-chemical
route. Encourage predators to take up residence

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:27.080
in your yard. Birds and bats will eat thousands
of pests during the day and night. Predatory

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:32.270
bugs like lacewings, ladybugs, mantids, dragonflies,
and centipedes can target specific pests and

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:34.330
can be ordered commercially for release in
your yard.

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:38.070
Narrator: Certain viruses or molds will only
kill one species of pest. They can be applied

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:42.400
similarly to more traditional pesticides.
Narrator: So, David, I think you've learned

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:45.570
a lot here. What do you think you're going
to do about your penguin problem?

00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:50.580
David: I think I'm going to follow your
advice and try a chemical free strategy.

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:57.580
Narrator: Oh? Which is it?
[penguin sounds][sound of penguins scattering

00:12:05.690 --> 00:12:12.690
away] [sound of polar bear growling]

